From a903b82d514b817ae96894a9719e851cca8ef35a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yekai pan <pyk@seas.upenn.edu>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 04:10:28 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] l2r-local: issue 38: tagged FFI objects: clear the top 16
 bits before the inline count access

A `tagged` opaque handle (`#[ffi(rust = "path", tagged)]`, patch 0050) is
either an odd immediate or a pointer to a box whose `u32` count is at
offset 0. On 64-bit targets the foreign side may now also keep data in the
top 16 bits of a pointer handle: `rc.inc` clears them (`llvm.ptrmask` with
2^48 - 1, provenance kept) before it loads and stores the count, on every
path (plain, with a delta, atomic). The cleanup hook still gets the handle
unchanged, so the foreign side sees its own encoding. lean2rr's one-word
`Box` (`leanrt::any::LAny`) keeps the number of its payload's type there,
as `lean_object*`-style runtimes keep tags; without the mask the increment
reads and writes an address with those bits set (a SIGSEGV on aarch64,
which ignores only the top 8 bits, and on x86-64).

Nothing changes for the existing tagged types (lean2rr's `Nat` and `Int`):
their pointers come from mimalloc and have the top 16 bits clear (user
addresses are below 2^48 on aarch64 and x86-64), so the mask is a no-op on
them. It costs one AND on the increment of a big number (the even-word
path only; small values never reach it). Untagged objects and targets
narrower than 64 bits lower as before. No other code reads through a
tagged handle: `rc.dec` calls the hook, the nonlinear-FFI instrumentation
and the unique-carrying analysis skip tagged objects, and an ffi_object
gets no reuse token and no deferred release (patch 0050's notes).

Tests: conversion/tagged_ffi_object.mlir (the mask before the load and the
store, also with a delta and before the atomicrmw of an atomic count; none
for an untagged object; the hook gets the unmasked handle).
